install-init.sh has no path for Debian, Fedora or Manjaro

The dispatcher matches only `ubuntu|linuxmint`, `amzn` and `arch` against ID in
/etc/os-release. Debian, Fedora and ID=manjaro all fall off the end of the chain.

The immediate symptom was fixed during the container audit: the /etc/os-release read is now
guarded and an unsupported platform fails loudly, where before `make install` printed three
"No such file or directory" lines and exited 0 having installed nothing. The underlying gap
remains.

Manjaro and Debian are cheap: Manjaro is Arch, and Debian is close enough to Ubuntu that
both are mostly a matter of adding the ID to the existing arm. Worth checking the Ubuntu
installer's PPA use before claiming Debian support, since add-apt-repository ppa: does not
apply there.

Fedora is a larger piece of work. install-amazon-linux.sh is the dnf-based starting point,
but the package names differ and the EPEL handling does not carry over: on Amazon Linux
`rpm -E %{rhel}` returns the literal string %{rhel} and AL2023 publishes no epel-release
at all, so the EPEL attempt was removed rather than fixed. Fedora needs the opposite
treatment.
